Most commands accept extra options called arguments, written as a hyphen plus the option (a flag/switch), like the -c in ping -c. By default, a command just does its basic job. For example, ls alone lists files but skips hidden ones. We can add an argument to change that behavior.

 

Using our ls example, ls informs us that there is only one folder named "folder1" as highlighted in the snippet below.


Using ls to view the contents of a directory
tryhackme@linux2:~$ ls
folder1
tryhackme@linux2:~$
However, after using the -a argument (short for --all), we now suddenly have an output with a few more files and folders such as ".hiddenfolder". Files and folders with "." are hidden files.


Using ls to view hidden folders
tryhackme@linux2:~$ ls -a 
.hiddenfolder folder1
tryhackme@linux2:~$
Commands that allow arguments to be used will often have a --help argument. This argument will list the possible options that the command accepts, provide a brief description and example of how to use it.


Listing the options we can use with ls
tryhackme@linux2:~$ ls --help
